INT. SKATING RINK CONCESSION STAND - AFTERNOON

1989. Faded posters of rollerskates. A disco ball turns slowly over empty hardwood. The popcorn machine hums. Behind the counter, a cluttered shelf of mismatched rental skates.

JUNIPER "JUNE" ABARA, 16, braids and a Walkman, counts quarters into a cash drawer. She is the only one working. Her mouth moves silently with whatever she hears.

The front door chimes. DANNY KOSTOPOULOS, 17, enters. Letterman jacket too big for him. He carries a gym bag.

DANNY
Hey. Is the big room open?

JUNE
(pulling off a headphone)
Not till four.

DANNY
I'll wait.

JUNE
Suit yourself.

He sits on a stool. Taps the counter. Pretends not to look at her. She pretends not to notice.

DANNY
Do you work here, like, always?

JUNE
Saturdays. And school nights. And whenever Mr. Pruitt forgets to pay somebody else.

DANNY
That sounds rough.

JUNE
It's okay. I get free popcorn.

She slides a small bag across the counter. He takes it.

DANNY
Thanks.

JUNE
Don't tell Pruitt.

DANNY
Wouldn't.

They sit in friendly quiet. The disco ball turns. A speaker crackles overhead.

MR. PRUITT (V.O.)
(over the PA)
June, did you restock the nacho cheese?

JUNE
(calling up at the ceiling)
Yes, Mr. Pruitt!

MR. PRUITT (V.O.)
Good girl.

June rolls her eyes. Danny almost laughs. He pulls a folded paper from his jacket.

DANNY
Can I ask you something? Not weird.

JUNE
That's how weird questions start.

DANNY
It's about my sister.

JUNE
Okay.

He unfolds the paper. A flyer. A missing-person flyer, photocopied crooked. A girl's face, braids like June's.

DANNY
She worked here. Last summer.

June stops counting quarters.

JUNE
Rosalind.

DANNY
You knew her?

JUNE
I took her shifts. When she left.

DANNY
She didn't leave. She --

He cuts himself off. June closes the cash drawer. Very slowly.

JUNE
Danny. Mr. Pruitt keeps a locked room. Behind the skate lockers.

DANNY
What's in it?

JUNE
I don't know. But Rosalind's jacket is hanging on the door.

Danny stares at her. The popcorn machine hums. The disco ball spins across his face.

DANNY
Show me.

JUNE
Not now.

DANNY
Why not?

MR. PRUITT (V.O.)
(over the PA, cheerful)
Folks, we're opening the big room early today. Skate on in!

The doors at the far end of the rink swing open. Light floods the floor.

JUNE
That's why.

She reaches under the counter. Pulls out a ring of keys. Presses them into his palm.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Four o'clock. When the music's loud. The brass one.

DANNY
(barely a whisper)
Thank you.

JUNE
Don't thank me yet.

Danny pockets the keys. Picks up his gym bag. Skaters spill through the far doors. June puts on her Walkman and turns back to the register, calm as a clock.

The disco ball catches Danny's face one last time as he walks into the crowd.
